{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 5,
  "estimated_minutes": 40,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand Syncora’s insurance of securitized HELOC payments, its third-party-beneficiary rights against EMC, and its request for threshold burden-of-proof rulings rather than adjudication of actual warranty breaches."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "New York Insurance Law § 3106 supplies an increased-risk principle rather than an independent damages remedy, while the REMIC restrictions explain why SSA § 2.05(a) regulates repurchase of performing loans."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "Understanding the MLPA § 7 construction requires connecting its insurer-interest trigger with I&I §§ 3.01(c) and 5.01(a) and the nondefaulting-loan provision in SSA § 2.05(a)."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The holdings are explicit, but the reviewer must distinguish the insurance-law analogy and supporting precedent from any ruling on actual breaches, damages causation, or entitlement to rescission-equivalent relief."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The increased-risk reasoning links d01 and d02, while d03 and d04 share the same SSA passage and must be distinguished as whole-transaction coherence versus construction of the supporting SSA provision."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d03 — It requires integrating the MLPA repurchase trigger, the I&I Event of Default, and the SSA’s tax-opinion condition for repurchasing nondefaulting loans. The reviewer must also distinguish an I&I contractual default from a borrower’s HELOC default and recognise that the SSA restriction supports, rather than independently establishes, the repurchase remedy.",
  "reason": "Assign this to a reviewer comfortable tracing related transaction documents, though securitization expertise is not essential. The central dispute is readily stated: Syncora relies on increased risk at closing, whereas EMC demands a causal connection to loan defaults or actual loss. The main work is understanding why that distinction applies separately to the I&I material-breach claim and the MLPA repurchase trigger, and how the SSA supports the latter construction. The opinion supplies the necessary insurance and REMIC background and states its holdings clearly, keeping the case below hard despite the cross-document reasoning."
}
